      en: "Puncak Jaya, also known as Carstensz Pyramid, is the highest mountain in Indonesia and the entire continent of Oceania, standing at 4,884 meters. It is one of the world's 'Seven Summits'—the highest peaks on each continent—making it a legendary goal for mountain climbers. Located in the Sudirman Range of Papua, its jagged limestone ridges are surrounded by the world's largest gold and copper mine, the Grasberg. Puncak Jaya is extremely unique for possessing several small glaciers, though they are melting rapidly due to climate change. The peak was first named after the Dutch explorer Jan Carstensz, who was mocked in Europe for claiming to see snow near the Equator in 1623. Geography K10 — Glaciology and the Seven Summits."
