      en: "Mount Semeru, also known as 'Mahameru' (The Great Mountain), is the highest peak on the island of Java and one of the world's most active volcanoes. Located in East Java within the Bromo-Tengger-Semeru National Park, this massive stratovolcano is legendary for its near-constant activity, emitting a plume of ash and steam every 20 to 30 minutes. In Javanese Hindu cosmology, Semeru is believed to be the 'Nail of Java,' brought from India to stabilize the island. The trek to its 3,676-meter summit is a popular but dangerous journey for experienced climbers, passing through the beautiful Ranukumbolo lake and a steep, sandy slope of volcanic ash. It represents the raw, creative, and destructive power of the tectonic forces that built Indonesia. Geography K7 — Tectonic Activity and Active Stratovolcanoes."
