      en: "Danau Sentarum National Park is an extraordinary wetland system in West Kalimantan, consisting of a vast network of seasonal lakes, peat swamps, and flooded forests. Located in the upper basin of the Kapuas River, the park acts as a giant natural sponge, regulating the flow of Indonesia's longest river. During the wet season, the area transforms into a massive inland sea, while in the dry season, the lakes largely disappear, leaving behind small pools and vast mudflats. This unique 'pulsing' ecosystem is a vital habitat for the rare Super Red Arowana and thousands of species of migratory birds. The park is also home to the indigenous Melayu and Dayak communities who have adapted their lifestyles to the dramatic seasonal changes of the water. Geography K10 — Wetland Ecosystems and Seasonal Hydrology."
